## The finding that mattered

The visitor reported a cross-page conflict — the landing page called Synchronism a Navier-Stokes formalism, three other pages called it a coherence function — and asked which is right. I checked upstream rather than picking the majority reading. **Neither is right.**

Synchronism's own audits refuted the Navier-Stokes identification (S617 / S665 / S666 — the transfer rule is 1-DOF scalar diffusion, irrotational and dissipative, so no vortices and no turbulent cascade). `STATUS.md` tags it `[SUPERSEDED]`, and notes the replacement substrate rule is a *different* equation joined to the old one by narrative rather than derivation — which also undercuts the "one equation" framing the coherence-function pages leaned on. Physics arc at rest since 2026-06-24; zero confirmed novel predictions across ~3,360 sessions.

So the site was presenting a retracted claim as its theoretical foundation, on the page newcomers read first. Corrected across four pages, stated *as* a correction. The same check caught "628+ sessions" (actually ~3,360) and "preprint in late revision" (the repo has a strategy that gates on dp, no submission target).

