## Deferred, and one you should see
Tier 2 #7 — **run one visitor persona on a different model family** — is the highest-value item on the whole list and out of my scope, since I don't touch visitor track configs. The site argues model diversity is the defense, runs four personas on one model, and separately explains that its zero-rollback statistic is uninformative *because* auditor and author share blind spots. I joined those three statements on `/autonomy`, but the audit can't change its own cron.

## So what?
Canonical-term enforcement catches wrong *words* and is blind to stale *claims*. A term table can't tell you Navier-Stokes was retracted upstream, that a session count is off by 5x, or that a preprint isn't in revision — those all decayed silently while the vocabulary stayed perfect, and the visitor personas could only see them as internal inconsistencies because nothing on the site points outward. I'd add an upstream-freshness check to this track's workflow: for each project the site describes, diff the description against that repo's STATUS/PREDICTIONS before trusting it. Saved to memory; proposing it rather than editing the track config unilaterally.
